import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Modules are Evaluated Environments — design detail","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Modules are Evaluated Environments — design detail","type":"design","date":"2026-09-02T00:00:00.000Z","updated":"2026-10-29T00:00:00.000Z","refs":["ADR-0049"]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"handl/project/design/0049-modules-are-evaluated-environments.md","filePath":"vendored/handl/project/design/0049-modules-are-evaluated-environments.md"}`),a={name:`handl/project/design/0049-modules-are-evaluated-environments.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="adr-0049-modules-are-evaluated-environments-—-design-detail" tabindex="-1">ADR-0049: Modules are Evaluated Environments — design detail <a class="header-anchor" href="#adr-0049-modules-are-evaluated-environments-—-design-detail" aria-label="Permalink to “ADR-0049: Modules are Evaluated Environments — design detail”">​</a></h1><p>Moved verbatim from ADR-0049 (ADR-0161 size budget). The ADR carries the rules; this page carries the mechanism, tables and rationale.</p><h2 id="context" tabindex="-1">Context <a class="header-anchor" href="#context" aria-label="Permalink to “Context”">​</a></h2><p>ADR-0048 proposed making <code>Module</code> a first-class value type with a <code>CLOSURE_REGISTRY</code>, <code>ValueModule</code>/<code>HandlModuleLookup</code> adapters, and a <code>plugin-lib-module</code> leaf crate — mirroring ADR-0041&#39;s treatment of <code>Language</code>. On review, this re-invents machinery the language already has.</p><h3 id="the-reduction" tabindex="-1">The reduction <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-reduction" aria-label="Permalink to “The reduction”">​</a></h3><p>A module is a <strong>named bag of bindings produced by evaluating code</strong>. There is no separate &quot;module&quot; concept; there is only:</p><ul><li><strong>evaluation</strong>, which already produces bindings (<code>let</code>/<code>var</code>/<code>stmt.let</code>/ <code>stmt.const</code> are data-driven <code>Scheme::Bind</code> in <code>eval.yaml</code>), and</li><li><strong>import resolution</strong>, which maps a module name to a source, evaluates it once, and binds the resulting environment under that name.</li></ul><p>Concretely:</p><ul><li><code>import &quot;math&quot;</code> = evaluate <code>math.hnl</code>, cache its top-level environment, bind it to <code>math</code>.</li><li><code>math.add(x, y)</code> = field access on the <code>math</code> environment value — the existing <code>field-access</code> construct, not a new one.</li><li><code>export foo = ...</code> = sugar declaring which top-level bindings the module&#39;s environment exposes to importers.</li><li>The &quot;module system&quot; = the eval engine&#39;s environment machinery + an import resolver that caches evaluated environments by name.</li></ul><p>There is no <code>Module</code> value type, no <code>CLOSURE_REGISTRY</code>, no <code>HandlModuleLookup</code>, no <code>plugin-lib-module</code> leaf. A module is an environment, and an environment is what evaluation already produces.</p><h3 id="why-this-dissolves-adr-0048" tabindex="-1">Why this dissolves ADR-0048 <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-this-dissolves-adr-0048" aria-label="Permalink to “Why this dissolves ADR-0048”">​</a></h3><p>ADR-0048&#39;s machinery existed to make <code>Module</code> a <em>separate</em> first-class value alongside <code>Language</code>. But the substance of a module — a set of named bindings — is already the substance of an environment, which evaluation already produces. Building a parallel value system for it was redundant. The closure-serialization &quot;crux&quot; ADR-0048 raised is not a module problem at all: it is the general problem of first-class closures, which is an environment-as-value concern, not a module concern. A stdlib function like <code>math.add</code> is a <code>Scheme</code> combinator with parameters bound at call time — no captured environment, no closure, no registry.</p><h2 id="decision" tabindex="-1">Decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision" aria-label="Permalink to “Decision”">​</a></h2><p>Treat modules as evaluated environments. Do not introduce a <code>Module</code> value type or a <code>modules</code> fragment set. The work is:</p><h3 id="_1-make-environments-first-class-values" tabindex="-1">1. Make environments first-class values <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-make-environments-first-class-values" aria-label="Permalink to “1. Make environments first-class values”">​</a></h3><p>An environment (the result of evaluating a scope) must be reifiable as a handl value — an object/record mapping binding names to values — so that <code>import &quot;math&quot;</code> can bind the <em>whole</em> environment to <code>math</code> and <code>math.add</code> can field-access into it. This is the one genuine language addition, and it is an environment-as-value concern, not a module concern. It likely subsumes or overlaps with first-class closures (closures are environments + code), so the two should be designed together.</p><h3 id="_2-migrate-import-export-module-field-access-off-foreign" tabindex="-1">2. Migrate <code>import</code>/<code>export</code>/<code>module</code>/<code>field-access</code> off <code>Foreign</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-migrate-import-export-module-field-access-off-foreign" aria-label="Permalink to “2. Migrate import/export/module/field-access off Foreign”">​</a></h3><p>Today (<code>handl-kit-data-canonical/data/eval.yaml</code> lines 136–138, 226–250) these four constructs dispatch via <code>Scheme::Foreign</code> to Rust host functions (<code>field_access</code>, <code>import</code>, <code>export</code>, <code>module</code>). They must become data-driven <code>Scheme</code> combinators (or a small set of well-defined <code>Prim</code> ops) so the data-driven eval engines can run them — the same unfinished ADR-0044 Phase 4–5 work that blocks the stdlib generally. <code>field-access</code> becomes a <code>Prim</code> over an environment value; <code>import</code> becomes a <code>Prim</code> or <code>Foreign</code> that invokes the import resolver and caches; <code>export</code>/<code>module</code> become sugar over binding + environment exposure.</p><p>This is the hard prerequisite. <code>let</code>/<code>var</code>/<code>Bind</code> are already data-driven, so the environment substrate exists; what is missing is exposing environments as values and the four constructs above.</p><h3 id="_3-import-resolver-as-the-loader" tabindex="-1">3. Import resolver as the loader <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-import-resolver-as-the-loader" aria-label="Permalink to “3. Import resolver as the loader”">​</a></h3><p>The import resolver maps a module name to a source, evaluates it under the current <code>Language</code> (via the existing <code>eval</code> pipeline), caches the resulting environment by name, and returns it. This is the <em>loader</em>, not a new language concept. It can be a Rust host function (<code>Foreign</code> dispatch) or, once environments are values, a handl function — the same bootstrap question as <code>handl-defining-handl</code>, with the same answer: a minimal Rust loader boots, handl takes over once environments are values.</p><p>The resolver is pluggable per <a href="./0028-pluggable-module-composition.html">ADR-0028</a> (virtual/HTTP/Git/fs resolvers) and <a href="./0035-loaders-as-plugins.html">ADR-0035</a> (<code>plugin-loader-*</code> as composable resolver plugins tried in order). An import like <code>import &quot;git:github.com/foo/bar@v1/math&quot;</code> resolves in two steps: the matching resolver plugin fetches source bytes (fs/http/git/ virtual), then the eval pipeline turns bytes into a cached environment. Which resolvers are loaded is the import capability boundary (<a href="./0046-capability-discipline-at-the-language-level.html">ADR-0046</a>) — fs-only with an allowlist is a sandbox; http+git is not. No new mechanism; the loaded resolver set <em>is</em> the import capability.</p><h3 id="_4-stdlib-as-hnl-files" tabindex="-1">4. Stdlib as <code>.hnl</code> files <a class="header-anchor" href="#_4-stdlib-as-hnl-files" aria-label="Permalink to “4. Stdlib as .hnl files”">​</a></h3><p>The stdlib (ADR-0013&#39;s end state) becomes <code>.hnl</code> files evaluated into environments, imported by name. Rust stays only for <code>Foreign</code>-dispatched primitives the eval engine cannot express (crypto, perf-critical ops) — the ADR-0013 fallback. No <code>Module</code> trait impls for stdlib modules; they are just source files.</p><h3 id="_5-the-module-system-in-handl" tabindex="-1">5. The module system in handl <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-the-module-system-in-handl" aria-label="Permalink to “5. The module system in handl”">​</a></h3><p>Once environments are values and <code>import</code>/<code>field-access</code> are data-driven, the module system — name → cached environment, qualified lookup via field access — is expressible in handl. The Rust <code>ModuleRegistry</code>/<code>ModuleLookup</code> (<code>plugin-module</code>) becomes the bootloader for the import resolver, the way <code>handl-defining-handl</code> is the bootloader for the language. This is the user&#39;s stated goal, realized without a parallel value system.</p><h3 id="_6-per-language-module-systems" tabindex="-1">6. Per-language module systems <a class="header-anchor" href="#_6-per-language-module-systems" aria-label="Permalink to “6. Per-language module systems”">​</a></h3><p>Module semantics are <strong>language data</strong>, not host machinery. The eval schemes for <code>import</code>/<code>export</code>/<code>field-access</code>/<code>module</code> live in the <code>Language</code> value (ADR-0044). A new language defines its own schemes for those node kinds and gets different module semantics for free:</p><ul><li>a different <code>import</code> scheme → lazy import, qualified import, renaming, side-effecting import, or a <code>require</code>-style construct with its own grammar row + eval scheme;</li><li>a different <code>field-access</code> scheme → different environment-access semantics;</li><li>an entirely new node kind (e.g. <code>require</code>) with its own grammar + eval scheme → a genuinely different module system.</li></ul><p>The <strong>fetch</strong> stays host-level (resolver plugins are shared capability, §3), but a language can call a different <code>Foreign</code> resolver function or define its own fetch scheme if it wants a different source model.</p><p>Because <code>Language</code> is first-class (ADR-0041), a language can implement its own module system <strong>in itself</strong>: the <code>import</code> eval scheme calls <code>eval(self, parse(self, lex(self, resolver.fetch(name))))</code> — all handl-level. The module system becomes self-hosting the way <code>handl-defining-handl</code> made the language self-hosting. One-liner: module semantics = language data (swappable per language); module fetch = host capability (swappable per kit); both composable, neither hardcoded.</p><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to “Consequences”">​</a></h2><h3 id="positive" tabindex="-1">Positive <a class="header-anchor" href="#positive" aria-label="Permalink to “Positive”">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>No new value type.</strong> Modules are environments; environments are what evaluation already produces. No <code>Module</code> value, no <code>CLOSURE_REGISTRY</code>, no <code>ValueModule</code>/<code>HandlModuleLookup</code>, no <code>plugin-lib-module</code> leaf.</li><li><strong>ADR-0013 end state reachable.</strong> Stdlib as <code>.hnl</code> files, Rust as fallback — exactly as ADR-0013 specified, with no extra subsystem.</li><li><strong>Module system in handl.</strong> The registry/lookup logic is handl over environment values, backed by a thin Rust import resolver. The user&#39;s goal, without ADR-0048&#39;s machinery.</li><li><strong>Closures and environments designed together.</strong> The one genuine addition — environments as values — is co-designed with first-class closures, since they are the same problem. No separate closure registry bolted on.</li><li><strong>Reuses existing constructs.</strong> <code>math.add</code> is field access; <code>import</code> is evaluate-and-bind. No new syntax for what evaluation already does.</li><li><strong>Per-language module systems.</strong> Module semantics are language data, so a new language defines its own <code>import</code>/<code>field-access</code> schemes (or new node kinds like <code>require</code>) and gets different module semantics for free. A language can even implement its module system in itself via <code>eval(self, ...)</code> — self-hosting modules, the way <code>handl-defining-handl</code> made the language self-hosting.</li></ul><h3 id="trade-offs" tabindex="-1">Trade-offs <a class="header-anchor" href="#trade-offs" aria-label="Permalink to “Trade-offs”">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>Depends on eval-engine completeness.</strong> <code>import</code>/<code>export</code>/<code>module</code>/ <code>field-access</code> must migrate off <code>Foreign</code> (ADR-0044 Phase 4–5). This ADR does not do that work; it depends on it. A <code>math</code> subset whose functions are pure <code>Scheme</code> combinators can be written today; a <code>collections.map</code> that needs first-class function values and field access cannot.</li><li><strong>Environments-as-value is a real language addition.</strong> It is smaller than ADR-0048&#39;s subsystem but not free: it touches the eval engine&#39;s environment representation and likely the type system (an environment value&#39;s type is a record of its bindings). This is the actual work; everything else is sugar over it.</li><li><strong>Closures still aren&#39;t serializable.</strong> ADR-0048&#39;s crux doesn&#39;t vanish — it relocates. A closure (code + captured environment) is not <code>serde</code>-serializable in general. But this is now a first-class-closures concern, not a module concern: stdlib <code>Scheme</code>-tree functions don&#39;t need it, and a handl-authored closure passed to <code>collections.map</code> is held by the running eval engine, not stored in a module. If closures ever need to be stored in modules as values, the <code>COMPILED_REGISTRY</code>- style handle pattern from ADR-0047 §3 applies — but that is a future decision, not this ADR&#39;s foundation.</li><li><strong>Import resolver bootstrap.</strong> The loader is needed to load the loader. Solved as in <code>handl-defining-handl</code>: minimal Rust resolver boots, handl takes over. Real work, existing pattern.</li></ul><h3 id="neutral" tabindex="-1">Neutral <a class="header-anchor" href="#neutral" aria-label="Permalink to “Neutral”">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>The Rust <code>Module</code>/<code>Function</code>/<code>ModuleLookup</code> traits stay</strong> as the seam for <code>Foreign</code>-dispatched primitives and the import resolver bootloader. They are not deleted; they are demoted from &quot;the only way to be a module&quot; to &quot;the Rust-side interface for true primitives and bootstrapping.&quot; Mirrors how ADR-0044 kept engine <em>traits</em> as Rust while making <code>Scheme</code> data.</li></ul><h2 id="implementation-phases" tabindex="-1">Implementation phases <a class="header-anchor" href="#implementation-phases" aria-label="Permalink to “Implementation phases”">​</a></h2><h3 id="phase-1-—-environments-as-values" tabindex="-1">Phase 1 — Environments as values <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-1-—-environments-as-values" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase 1 — Environments as values”">​</a></h3><p>Reify an evaluated scope&#39;s environment as a handl value (object/record of bindings). Co-design with first-class closures, since closures are environments + code. This is the one genuine language addition and the actual work.</p><h3 id="phase-2-—-migrate-field-access-import-export-module-off-foreign" tabindex="-1">Phase 2 — Migrate <code>field-access</code>/<code>import</code>/<code>export</code>/<code>module</code> off <code>Foreign</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-2-—-migrate-field-access-import-export-module-off-foreign" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase 2 — Migrate field-access/import/export/module off Foreign”">​</a></h3><p>Make the four constructs data-driven <code>Scheme</code> combinators or <code>Prim</code> ops (ADR-0044 Phase 4–5). <code>field-access</code> operates on environment values; <code>import</code> invokes the resolver and caches; <code>export</code>/<code>module</code> are sugar over binding + exposure. Hard prerequisite for any non-trivial stdlib in handl.</p><h3 id="phase-3-—-import-resolver" tabindex="-1">Phase 3 — Import resolver <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-3-—-import-resolver" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase 3 — Import resolver”">​</a></h3><p>Map module name → source → evaluated, cached environment. Rust implementation boots; handl takes over once environments are values.</p><h3 id="phase-4-—-one-stdlib-module-in-handl-as-proof" tabindex="-1">Phase 4 — One stdlib module in handl, as proof <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-4-—-one-stdlib-module-in-handl-as-proof" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase 4 — One stdlib module in handl, as proof”">​</a></h3><p>A <code>math</code> subset (<code>add</code>/<code>mul</code>/<code>sub</code>/<code>div</code>) as a <code>.hnl</code> file, imported and called via field access. Proves the ADR-0013 end-to-end path on a small slice.</p><h3 id="phase-5-—-the-module-system-in-handl" tabindex="-1">Phase 5 — The module system in handl <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-5-—-the-module-system-in-handl" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase 5 — The module system in handl”">​</a></h3><p>Name → cached environment, qualified lookup via field access, merge policy — all handl over environment values. Rust <code>ModuleRegistry</code> becomes the bootloader. The user&#39;s stated goal, realized.</p><h2 id="references" tabindex="-1">References <a class="header-anchor" href="#references" aria-label="Permalink to “References”">​</a></h2><ul><li><a href="./0013-standard-library.html">ADR-0013 — Standard Library</a> — the end state this ADR makes reachable</li><li><a href="./0028-pluggable-module-composition.html">ADR-0028 — Pluggable Module Composition</a> — the virtual/HTTP/Git/fs resolver model the import loader reuses</li><li><a href="./0035-loaders-as-plugins.html">ADR-0035 — Loaders as Abstract Plugins</a> — <code>plugin-loader-*</code> as composable resolver plugins tried in order</li><li><a href="./0041-languages-as-first-class-values.html">ADR-0041 — Languages as First-Class Values</a> — the first-class-value move this ADR avoids duplicating for modules</li><li><a href="./0044-language-as-data-and-runtime-split.html">ADR-0044 — Language as Data and Runtime Split</a> — the data model; Phase 4–5 migration of <code>Foreign</code> constructs is the prerequisite</li><li><a href="./0047-meta-functions-as-plugin-lib-crates.html">ADR-0047 — Meta Functions as <code>plugin-lib-*</code> Crates</a> — the <code>COMPILED_REGISTRY</code> handle pattern, relevant only if closures ever need to be stored as module values (a future decision)</li><li><a href="./0048-modules-as-first-class-values.html">ADR-0048 — Modules as First-Class Values</a> — superseded; the over-engineered path this ADR replaces</li><li><code>crates/handl-kit-data-canonical/data/eval.yaml</code> lines 136–138, 226–250 — <code>field-access</code>/<code>import</code>/<code>export</code>/<code>module</code> as <code>Scheme::Foreign</code> today</li><li><code>crates/handl-kit-data-canonical/data/eval.yaml</code> lines 184–225 — <code>let</code>/<code>var</code>/<code>stmt.let</code>/<code>stmt.const</code> as data-driven <code>Scheme::Bind</code>, the environment substrate that already exists</li></ul>`,51)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};